  "step_3": "The decision reason requires two components: (1) treating probability differences as negligible, and (2) preferring the lottery with the higher maximum outcome. The individual does consider the maximum outcome as a desirable feature (favoring A), which aligns with the second part of the reason. However, they explicitly acknowledge the higher probability of winning in B, indicating they do not treat probability differences as negligible—contrary to the decision reason’s rule. Therefore, while the focus on the highest amount shows partial alignment, the failure to treat probabilities as negligible means the full reasoning rule was not applied. The individual appears to weigh both probability and outcome, rather than dismissing probability differences as the reason prescribes.",
  "final_assessment_score": 30
}

